 Recent work has explored large language models (LLMs) for zero-shot complaint classification. In zero-shot settings, models receive the candidate categories in the prompt and must return a valid label without task-specific training. This is attractive for complaint triage because new product taxonomies or institutional routing schemes may be introduced before large labelled datasets are available. However, zero-shot classification also raises practical issues: models can confuse overlapping financial products, output labels outside the allowed taxonomy, and show sensitivity to prompt and deployment details. Once a local zero-shot baseline is established for a given open-weight model, the next methodological question is how much performance can be gained through parameter-efficient fine-tuning under a fair and leakage-controlled protocol.
 
-This paper addresses that question. The central novelty is not a new model architecture or a new fine-tuning algorithm. Instead, the contribution is a reproducible applied evaluation and leakage-safe benchmark extension of a zero-shot experimental setting. We introduce a duplicate-aware train/validation/test split, exclude all legacy zero-shot subsets from model selection and training, and evaluate paired zero-shot versus QLoRA fine-tuned models in the same Hugging Face pipeline. Unlike single-model or single-seed complaint-classification studies, the present work evaluates the zero-shot-to-fine-tuned transition under paired, multi-seed, multi-model, leakage-controlled conditions. Two different 8B-class open-source model families, Qwen3-8B and Llama 3.1-8B, are evaluated across three random seeds. The final evaluation uses both a held-out internal test set and preserved legacy zero-shot subsets.
+Prior work already establishes the relevance of LLMs to this task. In particular, G\"ull\"u et al. fine-tuned GPT-2 and Llama 3.1-8B for complaint classification, including a five-category financial-complaint dataset \cite{gullu2025complaintllm}, while Roumeliotis et al. evaluated zero-shot LLM and reasoning-model classification on the same financial taxonomy \cite{roumeliotis2025think}. Consequently, neither the use of an LLM for financial complaint classification nor supervised adaptation of Llama 3.1-8B is introduced here.
+
+The present study instead examines the within-model transition from zero-shot prompting to explicitly specified 4-bit QLoRA adaptation under a fixed training budget. Qwen3-8B and Llama 3.1-8B are each compared before and after adaptation using the same classification prompt and Hugging Face inference pipeline, with three fine-tuning seeds per model family. An internal test partition and preserved balanced and imbalanced legacy subsets provide complementary evaluation views. This design is intended to quantify adaptation gains, seed variation, and the trade-offs against lighter supervised classifiers; it does not establish a new architecture, a new complaint corpus, or classification superiority over prior studies. Full-narrative grouping is also distinguished from model-visible input separation, whose remaining limitations are stated in Section~\ref{sec:limitations}.
 
 The contributions are as follows:
 \begin{enumerate}
-    \item We provide a leakage-controlled transition from zero-shot open-source LLM inference to QLoRA fine-tuning for CFPB consumer complaint classification.
-    \item We preserve pre-existing zero-shot subsets as a legacy benchmark and explicitly exclude them from fine-tuning and model selection.
-    \item We compare base zero-shot and fine-tuned models within the same Hugging Face quantised inference pipeline, isolating the effect of fine-tuning from infrastructure differences.
-    \item We evaluate two 8B-class model families, Qwen3-8B and Llama 3.1-8B, each across seeds 42, 43, and 44.
-    \item We report internal test performance, legacy benchmark performance, paired Wilcoxon tests, confidence intervals, latency, off-label behaviour, and class-level error patterns.
+    \item We specify a fixed-budget, completion-only QLoRA protocol for five-label complaint classification with generated JSON outputs.
+    \item We compare base zero-shot and adapted models within the same quantised Hugging Face inference pipeline, keeping the prompt and evaluation examples fixed within each comparison.
+    \item We evaluate Qwen3-8B and Llama 3.1-8B across fine-tuning seeds 42, 43, and 44, separating the adaptation comparison from model-family ranking.
+    \item We retain the earlier balanced and imbalanced zero-shot subsets alongside an internal test partition, and examine the dependence and input-overlap limitations of this evaluation design.
+    \item We assess the adaptation gains alongside TF-IDF and encoder baselines, class-level errors, off-label outputs, and the measured inference cost.
 \end{enumerate}
 
 \section{Related work}
 
 Classical consumer complaint classification has relied on sparse lexical features, support vector machines, logistic regression, and later transformer encoders. BERT-style models enabled strong supervised text classification by adapting contextual representations to task-specific labels \cite{devlin2019bert}; BERT, DistilBERT, and RoBERTa remain important supervised baselines for complaint categorisation \cite{devlin2019bert,sanh2019distilbert,liu2019roberta,sy2024beyondbert}. Complaint analytics has also been studied across food-industry categorisation, telecom complaint prediction, multicriteria complaint prioritisation, CFPB topic modelling, and financial-complaint interpretability, sentiment, cause, emotion, and severity analysis \cite{bozyigit2022foodcomplaints,gewaly2024telecomcomplaint,vairetti2024complaintpriority,raju2022cfpbtopic,das2024negative}. These studies show that supervised learning can be effective for complaint taxonomies, but they do not directly address how modern open-weight generative models should be adapted and evaluated for the same task.
 
-Recent work has made LLMs relevant to both complaint generation and complaint processing. Large-scale studies report that consumers increasingly use LLMs to draft clearer financial complaints \cite{shin2025adoption,liang2025widespread}, suggesting that complaint corpora are themselves being reshaped by generative AI. In parallel, LLM-based complaint classification frameworks use generative models for information extraction, customer-service benchmarking, and fine-tuned complaint classification \cite{yuan2024framework,li2025teleeval,gullu2025complaintllm}. Closest to this study, Roumeliotis et al. evaluated 14 standard and reasoning LLMs for zero-shot CFPB complaint classification into five financial categories \cite{roumeliotis2025think}. That work demonstrates the promise of zero-shot inference, but it focuses on a balanced 1000-record setting and does not address task-specific adaptation, multi-seed stability, or paired evaluation on both balanced and naturally imbalanced benchmarks.
-
-PEFT provides a middle path between zero-shot inference and full fine-tuning. LoRA learns low-rank adapter matrices while freezing pretrained weights \cite{hu2022lora}, and QLoRA enables quantised LLM adaptation through 4-bit NormalFloat quantisation, double quantisation, and LoRA adapters \cite{dettmers2023qlora}. Other PEFT methods such as prefix tuning and $(IA)^3$ are also viable \cite{li2021prefix,liu2022fewshot}, but QLoRA was selected here because it is well supported for 4-bit adaptation of 8B-class instruction models on 16 GB GPUs. The remaining methodological gap is how to connect zero-shot open-source LLM inference with supervised adaptation under a fair protocol: whether locally deployable models can be moved into PEFT without leakage, whether gains are stable across seeds and model families, whether preserved zero-shot subsets can remain valid final benchmarks, and how the adapted models compare with lighter classical and encoder baselines. This paper addresses that applied evaluation gap rather than proposing a new LoRA or QLoRA algorithm.
+Recent work has made LLMs relevant to both complaint generation and complaint processing. Large-scale studies report that consumers increasingly use LLMs to draft clearer financial complaints \cite{shin2025adoption,liang2025widespread}, suggesting that complaint corpora are themselves being reshaped by generative AI. LLM-based frameworks have also been investigated for complaint categorisation and customer-service benchmarking \cite{yuan2024framework,li2025teleeval}. Two studies are particularly close to the present work and require separate comparisons.
+
+G\"ull\"u et al. \cite{gullu2025complaintllm} compare CNN, LSTM, BERT, RoBERTa, GPT-2, and Llama 3.1-8B on two datasets: 5,629 Comcast complaints with rating labels and a second dataset reported to contain 162,421 financial complaints in five product categories. The latter categories correspond to the financial taxonomy studied here. Thus, their contribution is not restricted to telecommunications, and the present work cannot be distinguished merely by applying Llama to financial complaints. Their method uses an 80/20 train/test division; Table~V of their paper specifies three training epochs, a 512-token maximum, AdamW, cross-entropy loss, and weighted metric averaging for the LLMs. A LoRA or 4-bit quantisation configuration, a paired zero-shot baseline for each adapted checkpoint, and a three-seed uncertainty summary are not described in that report. Absence of these details is treated as a reporting difference, not evidence that an undocumented procedure was not performed.
+
+Our distinction from G\"ull\"u et al. is the adaptation question and the explicit protocol: 4-bit NF4 LoRA adaptation with completion-only loss for JSON label generation, a fixed 10,000-example training budget, two 8B model families evaluated before and after adaptation in the same inference pipeline, and repeated fine-tuning seeds. Table~\ref{tab:gullu-positioning} summarises these differences. Their published financial-dataset F1 scores should not be ranked against our macro-F1 scores: metric averaging, training budgets, and test construction differ. Nor does their published row count establish exact record-level identity with our 162,415-row source file.
