 Tables~\ref{tab:internal-summary} and~\ref{tab:bootstrap-ci} show large and statistically stable internal-test gains: macro-F1 improves by 0.2056 for Qwen3-8B and 0.2189 for Llama 3.1-8B, with bootstrap intervals indicating precise prediction-level improvements. Tables~\ref{tab:legacy-summary} and~\ref{tab:wilcoxon} provide the second layer of evidence. Because the legacy subsets are excluded from training and model selection, the Wilcoxon results across 12 subsets show that the gains are not merely internal-test artefacts but survive a protected return to the earlier zero-shot benchmark setting.
 
-Table~\ref{tab:supervised-baselines} prevents overclaiming: TF-IDF and encoder models remain strong supervised baselines for this fixed five-class taxonomy. The contribution is therefore not that PEFT universally dominates lighter models, but that it offers a locally deployable, leakage-controlled route from zero-shot open-source LLM inference to competitive supervised performance under a fixed training budget. This is useful when an organisation wants to keep the same generative model family for prompting, adaptation, and auditable local deployment rather than introducing a separate classifier for every downstream task. The latency results add the corresponding deployment trade-off: fine-tuning costs approximately 0.31--0.32 additional seconds per complaint, which is acceptable for offline analytics, audit, compliance, or semi-real-time routing but should still be considered alongside accuracy and reproducibility.
+Table~\ref{tab:supervised-baselines} shows that TF-IDF and encoder models remain strong supervised baselines for this fixed five-class taxonomy. Together with the prior LLM fine-tuning results of G\"ull\"u et al. \cite{gullu2025complaintllm}, this limits the contribution to the properties and trade-offs of the specified adaptation protocol rather than classification superiority or the first application of a fine-tuned LLM to financial complaints. The paired comparison quantifies the change from each model's own zero-shot performance, while the supervised baselines test whether that change justifies a more costly classifier for a fixed taxonomy. Retaining a generative model could support broader workflows, but taxonomy redefinition and cross-task reuse are not tested here. The measured additional inference time of approximately 0.31--0.32 seconds per complaint is a cost to report; its acceptability depends on workload requirements and is not established by these experiments. The different splits, budgets, and F1 averaging in G\"ull\"u et al. also preclude a numerical cross-paper superiority claim.
 
 Figure~\ref{fig:confusion-matrices} explains the main error-level change. In zero-shot mode, both models over-map \texttt{debt\_collection} narratives to \texttt{credit\_reporting}, which is plausible because debt disputes often mention credit reports, bureaus, disputed accounts, and collections together. Fine-tuning reduces this dominant error by approximately two thirds, suggesting that the adapters learn a more dataset-specific boundary. The remaining credit-reporting/debt-collection confusions also indicate where future deployment safeguards should focus: human review, cost-sensitive training, or class-specific calibration for high-impact boundary cases. This matters because the study evaluates product-category routing rather than consumer-facing decisions, but routing errors can still delay responses to particular complaint types.
 
 Finally, the results illustrate why leakage control is central to the comparison. If legacy zero-shot subsets had entered training, Tables~\ref{tab:legacy-summary} and~\ref{tab:wilcoxon} would no longer support a meaningful paired benchmark. By removing those records before split construction and using them only at final evaluation, the study evaluates the transition from zero-shot inference to QLoRA fine-tuning without erasing the benchmark that motivated the transition.
 
 \section{Limitations}
+\label{sec:limitations}
+
+The duplicate-aware design groups complete normalised narratives, but this does not guarantee separation of the 500-character inputs actually presented to the models. An audit of the archived data and Llama training-selection manifests identifies residual train/internal-test overlap after truncation and prompt construction. The legacy subsets also overlap with each other, so they cannot be treated as 12 independent replications. Consequently, the archived runs do not establish leakage-free evaluation; their results require model-visible overlap auditing and dependence-aware interpretation. These limitations must not be used as unsupported claims of methodological superiority over G\"ull\"u et al. \cite{gullu2025complaintllm}, whose reporting does not establish whether comparable audits were undertaken.
 
 This study does not propose a new architecture or PEFT algorithm; LoRA and QLoRA are established methods, and the contribution is a controlled empirical evaluation in a high-value application domain. The experiments use two 8B-class model families and a fixed 10,000-example training budget; larger decoder models, alternative PEFT methods, and larger training budgets remain future work. The zero-shot prompt is fixed rather than optimised, which supports paired comparison with the preserved zero-shot benchmark but does not rule out prompt sensitivity. The 500-character narrative limit keeps QLoRA training feasible on 16 GB GPUs and fully covers 63\% of examples, but longer context windows could benefit long-tail complaints. Encoder baselines could also be strengthened with domain-adapted checkpoints, larger variants, or wider hyperparameter searches. Latency is measured with serial batch-size-1 inference rather than deployment-oriented batching. Finally, the CFPB corpus is English-language and domain-specific, the split is not temporal, and the protected legacy subsets are separated from training and validation but still come from the same source distribution rather than an independently collected external dataset.
 
